    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Let us break down this interaction.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ну что, мы приехали?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Well what, we arrived?' The question word for 'what' comes right at the start, followed directly by 'we've arrived'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Да, мы на месте.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, we are in place.' Notice the word order: 'we' comes first, and 'place' finishes the sentence.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Нас уже ждут?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Are they waiting for us already?' Here 'us' sets up the idea, while 'are waiting' comes at the end for emphasis. Kostya answers straight away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Да, они нас ждут.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, they are waiting for us.' Here 'they' sets up the idea, while 'are waiting' comes at the end for emphasis.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Пошли узнаем, как дела.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Let's go find out how things are.' Listen for the word meaning 'let's go' right alongside 'will find out' in this line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Я немного волнуюсь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am a little worried.' Kostya starts with 'I' and pairs it with 'a bit' to make the point clear. Then Max replies.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ребята, послушайте меня.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Guys, listen to me.' Listen for the word meaning 'the boys' right alongside 'listen' in this line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я хочу вам что-то сказать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I want to tell you something.' In Russian, 'I' leads into 'tell' in a very natural everyday phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "В последние дни я перегибал палку.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'In the last days I went overboard.' The first content word means 'last', and 'stick' rounds out the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я вел себя очень плохо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I behaved very badly.' A compact expression built around 'I' and 'badly'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Мы понимаем тебя.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We understand you.' Listen for the word meaning 'we' right alongside 'to understand' in this line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Спасибо вам за поддержку.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Thank you for the support.' In Russian, 'thank you' leads into 'support' in a very natural everyday phrasing. Now let us listen to the conversation again without interruption.",
      "gap": 0.9
